@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Triggers Introduction";
}

<div class="container-fluid">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            SQL Server Triggers
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn what SQL Server Triggers are, why they are used,
            and how they automatically execute when database events occur.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Triggers Introduction
            </h2>

            <p>
                A <strong>Trigger</strong> is a special type of stored procedure
                that automatically executes when a specific event occurs
                in a SQL Server database.
            </p>

            <p>
                Unlike a normal stored procedure, a trigger is not executed
                directly using the <code>EXEC</code> statement.
                SQL Server automatically executes the trigger when its
                associated database event occurs.
            </p>

            <p class="mb-0">
                Triggers are commonly used to maintain data integrity,
                keep audit information, and automatically perform
                additional operations when data is inserted, updated,
                or deleted.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS A TRIGGER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is a Trigger?
            </h3>

            <p>
                A trigger is a database object that is automatically
                executed in response to a specific event on a table
                or other database object.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example, when a new student record is inserted
                into the <strong>Students</strong> table, an
                <strong>AFTER INSERT</strong> trigger can automatically
                execute another SQL statement.
            </p>

            <div class="alert alert-info">

                <strong>Important:</strong>

                A trigger runs automatically.
                You do not normally call it directly using
                <code>EXEC TriggerName</code>.

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHY TRIGGERS ARE USED
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Why are Triggers Used?
            </h3>

            <p>
                Triggers are useful when an operation should happen
                automatically after or instead of a database event.
            </p>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Automatically maintain related data.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Maintain audit information.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Track INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE operations.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Maintain data consistency.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Perform automatic actions when table data changes.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TRIGGER EVENTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Common Trigger Events
            </h3>

            <p>
                Triggers can be associated with different database
                operations.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Event</th>
                            <th>Description</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                <strong>INSERT</strong>
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Executes when a new record is inserted
                                into a table.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                <strong>UPDATE</strong>
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Executes when an existing record
                                is modified.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                        <tr>

                            <td>
                                <strong>DELETE</strong>
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Executes when a record is deleted
                                from a table.
                            </td>

                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TYPES OF TRIGGERS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Types of Triggers Covered
            </h3>

            <p>
                In this section we will learn the following triggers:
            </p>

            <div class="row g-3">

                <div class="col-md-6">

                    <div class="border rounded p-3 h-100">

                        <h5 class="fw-bold">
                            1. AFTER INSERT Trigger
                        </h5>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Executes after a new record has been
                            successfully inserted.
                        </p>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="col-md-6">

                    <div class="border rounded p-3 h-100">

                        <h5 class="fw-bold">
                            2. AFTER UPDATE Trigger
                        </h5>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Executes after an existing record
                            has been updated.
                        </p>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="col-md-6">

                    <div class="border rounded p-3 h-100">

                        <h5 class="fw-bold">
                            3. AFTER DELETE Trigger
                        </h5>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Executes after a record has been deleted.
                        </p>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="col-md-6">

                    <div class="border rounded p-3 h-100">

                        <h5 class="fw-bold">
                            4. INSTEAD OF Trigger
                        </h5>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Executes instead of the original
                            INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE operation.
                        </p>

                    </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Basic Trigger Example
            </h3>

            <p>
                Suppose we have a <strong>Students</strong> table
                and we want to automatically record an entry whenever
                a student is added.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TRIGGER trg_AfterInsertStudent
ON Students
AFTER INSERT
AS
BEGIN
    PRINT 'New student inserted successfully.';
END;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">

                <strong>Note:</strong>

                The above trigger automatically executes when an
                INSERT operation occurs on the <code>Students</code>
                table.

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A trigger executes automatically.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Triggers are associated with database events.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE are common trigger events.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Triggers can help maintain data consistency.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Triggers can be used for auditing and tracking changes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SQL Server supports different types of triggers
                    for different requirements.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary p-4 mb-4">

        <h3 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h3>

        <p class="mb-0">
            SQL Server Triggers are special database objects that
            automatically execute when specific database events occur.
            They are commonly used with INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE
            operations to maintain data integrity, track changes,
            and perform automatic database operations.
        </p>

    </div>


